WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.060
Your capstone action, your case story, and your 90-day plan.

2
00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:08.610
This is the video for module 4 of Client Relationship Management.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:21.490
Your capstone proves you can manage client relationships, not just describe them. It starts with a working tracker, and one proactive action you took with a real client, with her response.

4
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:30.720
Then you turn that work into a short case story with numbers, and finish with a 90-day plan, so the habits continue after the course.

5
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:36.980
Choose a Re-engage or Grow client from your monthly review, and read her notes first.

6
00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:39.470
A weak message just asks for work.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:52.110
A value-first message gives her something useful and specific, based on what she told you. You mentioned your shop anniversary is on 15 July. Would branded gift packs help?

8
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.230
This is why the records matter.

9
00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:05.270
For example, Tolu is a graphic designer in Abuja. Her notes say her client Grace wants to sell through a WhatsApp catalogue, but has no good food photos.

10
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:14.670
Tolu sends a free one-page guide to taking food photos in daylight with a phone, and offers to design matching price cards.

11
00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.210
Grace replies the same day and asks for a quote for 12 price cards.

12
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:27.450
Tolu quotes about 30,000 naira, Grace agrees, and Tolu logs the order and a new note.

13
00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:34.980
Then she asks Grace if she may use this exchange in her course project, without her name. Grace agrees.

14
00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:44.240
Not every action leads to an order. Record exactly what happened. An honest no order yet is better evidence than an invented success.

15
00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:50.400
Before you use any client's messages, ask her permission, even with her name hidden.

16
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:58.150
Blur names, phone numbers, profile photos and addresses in every screenshot, and call her Client A in your summary.

17
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.360
Share a copy or screenshot of your tracker, never the live file.

18
00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:10.970
Saying I am good with clients proves nothing. Everyone says it. A short case story with real numbers convinces people.

19
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:18.600
Situation. For example, Funmi had 46 clients, but no record of who had stopped buying.

20
00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:28.320
Action. She built a Google Sheets tracker, set reorder reminders from each client's buying gap, and started day-3 after-care checks.

21
00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:35.720
Result. Her retention went from 82.5 percent to about 89 percent the next quarter.

22
00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:45.710
She tells it three ways. For an employer, the numbers. For a partner, her reliability. For a shop client, simply: I remind you before you run out.

23
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:57.220
Relationship management is a routine, not a project you finish. Block 30 minutes in your calendar every week. Open the tracker and act on every red row.

24
00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:00.500
Log any orders not yet entered.

25
00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.110
Check promises due in the next seven days.

26
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:12.110
Send after-care checks for this week's deliveries, and reorder reminders for clients near the end of their usual gap.

27
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.920
On a bad week, do the 10-minute minimum: clear red rows and log orders.

28
00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:27.730
Your plan names one relationship to deepen. For example, Zainab in Kaduna chooses Blessing, a valuable client who went quiet.

29
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:37.640
It names a rhythm she will keep: after-care on day 3 for every client, and reminders 5 days before each client's usual gap ends.

30
00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:49.190
And it sets modest targets from her baseline of 70 percent retention: 75 percent or more by 31 December, and at least 3 new referred clients.

31
00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:56.150
Anyone could check on 31 December whether she did it. Share your plan with one person who will check on you.

32
00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:13.790
To remember. Give value first, using what you know about her. Protect her privacy in your evidence. Tell your story as Situation, Action, Result, with honest numbers. Keep your weekly 30 minutes. And make your 90-day plan specific enough to check.

33
00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:30.800
Now practise. Take one value-first action with a real client and save privacy-safe evidence. Write your case story for an employer and a client. Then write your 90-day plan, book your weekly review, and re-take the ten-point audit.

34
00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:33.090
Continue with the lessons below.
